<section xml:id="Section-1">
  <title>Great Expectations</title>

      <p>Fix a probability space <m>S</m> and <m>P: S \to [0,1]</m> a density function. 
      Then the set of random variables is just <m>\mathbb{R}^{S}</m>, is 
      the set of labelings of <m>S</m> by real-values. In particular, 
      the set of random variables is a vector space. There is a very 
      important map called the <alert>Expectation</alert>, which is</p>
      <me>\mathbb{E}[X] = \sum_{s \in S} X(s) P(s).</me>

      <lemma xml:id="lemma-linearity-expectation">
        <title>Linearity of Expectation</title>
        <statement>
          <p>The function <m>\mathbb{E}: \mathbb{R}^{S} \to \mathbb{R}</m> is a linear transformation.</p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>Let <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m> be random variables, and let <m>\lambda \in \mathbb{R}</m>. Then</p>
          <me>\mathbb{E}[\lambda X] = \dots =\lambda \mathbb{E}[X],</me>
          <p><fillin characters="72"/></p>
          <p>and</p>
          <me>\mathbb{E}[X + Y] = \dots =\mathbb{E}[X]+\mathbb{E}[Y].</me>
          <p><fillin characters="72"/></p>
        </proof>
      </lemma>

      <p>In fact, this extends more generally to infinite case using the same arguments but with infinite sums or more generally integrals. Expectation is one of the most useful linear transformations, and using linearity already buys you a lot.</p>

